[turn] round 1 AG1 main
  id: arg-a8c9e89573
Election Day should be a national holiday because school and work schedules currently create scheduling barriers that keep millions of Americans from voting; making Election Day a holiday would remove those barriers by giving people time to vote. Beyond making voting easier, treating Election Day as a celebration—rather than a chore—would elevate the importance of civic participation and democracy itself. This approach also has broad public support (71% of Democrats and 59% of Republicans) and aligns with established practice abroad, since 27 of 36 OECD countries hold elections on weekends or holidays.

[turn] round 1 AG2 defeat (undercut on Ass)
  id: arg-cbd7b0629a
  target: arg-a8c9e89573
  target_statement: Election Day should be a national holiday because school and work schedules currently create scheduling barriers that keep millions of Americans from voting; making Election Day a holiday would remove those barriers by giving people time to vote.
A “national holiday” designation at the federal level does not automatically give workers—especially those employed by private companies—the paid time off needed to vote. Federal law does not require private employers to treat Election Day as a day off or to pay workers for time to vote; in sectors like retail and other essential customer-facing services, employees often must work regardless. That means the very workers most likely to face rigid schedules and hourly pay—disproportionately low-income workers—may still be working on Election Day, or they may have to choose between voting and lost wages. So turning Election Day into a holiday is not a reliable way to remove the scheduling barriers the target highlights, and it can disadvantage the people who would benefit most from assured time to vote.

