[turn] round 1 AG2 counter (undercut on Ass)
  id: arg-36b8dd4817
  target: arg-3391e8d3a7
  target_statement: A holiday gives people protected time to vote, which directly addresses that scheduling constraint—so the claim that a holiday “by itself” is not an effective turnout strategy is mistaken.
A federal “national holiday” designation doesn’t reliably give workers protected time to vote, because federal law generally doesn’t force private employers to close or provide paid time off on that day. That means the scheduling barrier the target highlights can remain in practice—especially in retail and other essential service jobs where employees may still be scheduled. So the claim that a holiday directly supplies time to vote (and thus fixes the scheduling constraint) is not consistently true in the real workplace context.

