[turn] round 1 AG1 defeat (undercut on Ass)
  id: arg-a630640f39
  target: arg-36b8dd4817
  target_statement: A federal “national holiday” designation doesn’t reliably give workers protected time to vote, because federal law generally doesn’t force private employers to close or provide paid time off on that day.
The argument assumes that workers only get protected time to vote when federal law compels employers to close or provide paid time off. But a federal Election Day holiday would still change schedules in practice—schools would observe the holiday, and many workplaces normally operate on holiday schedules—so large numbers of people would have time to vote even without a universal employer-payout mandate. And because Election Day would be framed as a civic celebration (not just a day people must fit into a work shift), employers are more likely to accommodate voting hours, undercutting the claim that the scheduling barrier can “remain in practice” for most people.

